"This is absolutely unprecedented," Dr Quimby mutters as he fits Jones with a specialized stealth suit. "The temporal distortion readings from Sublime Point are off the charts. We're seeing reality itself becoming unstable within a five-kilometer radius."\
Jones grunts disinterestedly. He cares nothing for the scientific details, only for the mission parameters. "Will this suit work or not?"\
"The chrono-stabilizing fibers should provide some protection against the temporal fluctuations," Quimby says, adjusting a dial on Jones's wrist. "But I wouldn't recommend staying within the affected zone for more than six hours. After that, well..."\
"Well what?"\
Quimby grimaces. "You might experience some chrono-displacement. Aging decades in minutes, de-aging back to infancy, phasing partially into alternate timelines where you might be, say, a kindergarten teacher or a professional pastry chef."\
Jones glares with such intensity that Quimby takes several steps back.\
"Just stay within the mission timeframe and you'll be fine," the scientist adds hastily. "The insertion point we've identified should put you in a maintenance corridor near the central laboratory complex where Hardman was last seen."\
Jones inspects the weapons laid out before him: a standard-issue particle disruption pistol, a neural inhibitor, and a device labeled "Temporary Dimensional Anchor."\
"What's this last one do?"\
Quimby picks it up gingerly. "Think of it as a reality stabilizer. If the temporal distortions get too intense, activate this. It creates a bubble of stable spacetime for about ninety seconds\'97enough time to find cover or complete a critical action."\
Jones nods, concealing the devices about his person.\
"One last thing," Quimby says, handing him a small metal disc. "This is a specialized detection device. It will help you locate Hardman amidst the chaos. It's tuned to his specific biosignature."\
Jones accepts the disc, his expression hardening. "I don't need technology to find Hardman. I can smell his treachery."\
Quimby blinks several times. "Yes, well... the disc is a backup, then."\
As Jones leaves the equipment division, he passes the Department's chapel where the night shift agents have gathered for their pre-mission prayer. The sounds of a gospel hymn drift out, and Jones finds his steps slowing, drawn to the music.\
Horrified at himself, he quickens his pace, but not before the lines "though the darkness hide thee" have lodged themselves firmly in his mind.}